.TH ISODHCP 8 "December 2025" "Linux" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
isodhcp \- Unnumbered DHCP Server with Client Isolation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B isodhcp
[\fB\-i\fR \fIinterface\fR]
[\fB\-s\fR \fIserver_ip\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIpool_cidr\fR]
[\fB\-d\fR \fIdns_server\fR]
[\fB\-t\fR \fIlease_time\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR \fIlease_file\fR]
[\fB\-\-static\fR \fImac=ip[,hostname][,compat][,masquerade][,playground]\fR]...
[\fB\-\-dhcp\-option\fR \fICODE,TYPE,VALUE\fR]...
[\fB\-\-playground\-size\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-\-playground\-mac\fR \fImac\fR]...
[\fB\-\-playground\-oui\fR \fIprefix\fR]...
[\fB\-\-playground\-vendor\fR \fIstring\fR]...
[\fB\-\-compat\-mac\fR \fImac\fR]...
[\fB\-\-compat\-oui\fR \fIprefix\fR]...
[\fB\-\-compat\-vendor\fR \fIstring\fR]...
[\fB\-\-masquerade\-mac\fR \fImac\fR]...
[\fB\-\-masquerade\-oui\fR \fIprefix\fR]...
[\fB\-\-masquerade\-vendor\fR \fIstring\fR]...
[\fB\-\-nft\-set\-playground\fR \fIset_name\fR]
[\fB\-\-nft\-set\-playground\-subnet\fR \fIset_name\fR]
[\fB\-\-nft\-set\-isolated\fR \fIset_name\fR]
[\fB\-\-nft\-set\-compat\fR \fIset_name\fR]
[\fB\-\-nft\-set\-gateway\fR \fIset_name\fR]
[\fB\-\-nft\-set\-network\fR \fIset_name\fR]
[\fB\-\-nft\-set\-broadcast\fR \fIset_name\fR]
[\fB\-\-nft\-set\-subnet\fR \fIset_name\fR]...
[\fB\-\-hook\-file\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-isolation\fR \fI{on,off,system}\fR]
[\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-interval\fR \fIseconds\fR]
[\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-rate\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-max\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-candidates\fR \fIk\fR]
[\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-full\fR]
.PP
.B isodhcp
[\fB\-i\fR \fIinterface\fR]
{\fB\-\-status\fR | \fB\-\-verify\fR | \fB\-\-dump\fR[=\fIsections\fR] | \fB\-\-show\fR \fIclient\fR | \fB\-\-config\fR}
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
[\fB\-\-redact\fR]
[\fB\-\-all\fR]
[\fB\-\-socket\fR \fIpath\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B isodhcp
is a specialized DHCP server designed for secure Linux routers hosting guest,
IoT, or untrusted networks. It prioritizes Layer 3 client isolation and strictly
controls network topology based on client classification.

It operates in three distinct modes:

.B 1. Isolated Mode (Standard):
The default behavior. Assigns clients a \fB/32\fR subnet mask. The server
injects a specific host route into the kernel for each client. This forces all
traffic to pass through the gateway, enabling strict isolation.

.B 2. Micro-Segmentation Mode (Compatibility):
For "Legacy" devices that malfunction with /32 masks (e.g., game consoles), the
server dynamically allocates a tiny \fB/30\fR subnet (4 IPs). It creates a
secondary IP alias on the router interface to serve as the gateway for that
specific client.

.B 3. Playground Mode:
A reserved, contiguous subnet carved out of the main pool for devices that
require standard Layer 2 visibility (e.g., Chromecast, Apple TV). Clients in
the playground share a standard subnet mask (e.g., /26) and can communicate
with each other, even if global isolation is enforced.

.PP
Dynamic addresses are handed out from the top of the pool, which keeps it
compact for /30 blocks taken from the bottom. The choice is made among the four
highest free addresses, preferring the one that has been free the longest and
avoiding any that would break up a whole /30 block, so a just-released address
is not offered straight back out while an address free for longer is among the
four. A client that asks again after its lease ran out, or after releasing it,
gets its former address \(em or its /30 block \(em back if that is still
free. The release history is kept in the lease file.
.PP
The server features built-in DoS protection (quarantine), ARP conflict
detection, and robust integration with \fBnftables\fR for high-performance
firewalling.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-d\fR \fIdns\fR, \fB\-\-dns\fR \fIdns\fR
The DNS server IP address to offer clients. If omitted, the server parses
\fI/etc/resolv.conf\fR (and systemd-resolved upstream configurations) to find a
valid IPv4 nameserver.

.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fIpath\fR, \fB\-\-lease-file\fR \fIpath\fR
Path to the JSON file where lease state is persisted. Defaults to
\fIdhcp_leases_{INTERFACE}.json\fR in the current working directory. The server writes to
this file on allocation, release, and shutdown. To minimize disk wear, renewals
are only written if the expiration time changes significantly.

.TP
\fB\-i\fR \fIinterface\fR, \fB\-\-interface\fR \fIinterface\fR
The network interface to listen on (e.g., \fIeth1\fR, \fIwlan0\fR). The server
binds specifically to this interface to avoid conflicts with other DHCP servers
on the host. Defaults to \fIguest\fR.

.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fIpool\fR, \fB\-\-pool\fR \fIpool\fR
The CIDR range of IPs to allocate (e.g., \fI10.100.0.0/24\fR). If omitted, the
server attempts to auto-detect the subnet configured on the interface.

.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fIserver_ip\fR, \fB\-\-server-ip\fR \fIserver_ip\fR
The IP address of the router (gateway). If omitted, the server attempts to
auto-detect the primary IP address assigned to the interface specified
by \fB\-i\fR.

.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fIseconds\fR, \fB\-\-lease-time\fR \fIseconds\fR
The duration of the DHCP lease in seconds. Defaults to \fI3600\fR (1 hour).

.TP
\fB\-\-compat\-mac\fR \fImac\fR
Treat the specified MAC address as a "legacy" client. Instead of receiving the
standard strict \fB/32\fR netmask (client isolation), this device will be
assigned a \fB/30\fR micro-segment with a netmask of \fB255.255.255.252\fR).
.br
Useful for older printers, embedded devices, or game consoles that refuse to
accept a gateway outside of their own subnet mask.
.br
Example: \fI\-\-compat\-mac 00:11:22:33:44:55\fR

.TP
\fB\-\-compat\-oui\fR \fIoui\fR
Treat any device whose MAC address starts with this 3-byte prefix
(Organizationally Unique Identifier) as a legacy client.
.br
The OUI format is \fIAA:BB:CC\fR. Case is insensitive.
.br
Example: \fI\-\-compat\-oui B8:27:EB\fR (Raspberry Pi Foundation)

.TP
\fB\-\-compat\-vendor\fR \fIstring\fR
Treat any device sending a DHCP Vendor Class Identifier (option 60) containing
this substring as a legacy client.
.br
Useful for broad classes of devices or operating systems known to struggle with
unnumbered interfaces.
.br
Example: \fI\-\-compat\-vendor "MSFT 5.0"\fR

.TP
\fB\-\-masquerade\-mac\fR \fImac\fR
The device with this MAC will have their traffic source NAT'd (SNAT) by the
router. Traffic destined for these clients will appear to originate from the
gateway IP itself, rather than the original sender.

.TP
\fB\-\-masquerade\-oui\fR \fIprefix\fR
Apply source NAT to any device whose MAC address starts with this
3-byte prefix.

.TP
\fB\-\-masquerade\-vendor\fR \fIprefix\fR
Apply source NAT to any device that matches this DHCP Vendor Class Identifier.

.TP
\fB\-\-nft\-set\-playground\fR \fIname\fR
The daemon can populate named \fBNFTables\fR sets for integration with
firewall rules. Each option can only be given once and specifies an
optional name for a set (e.g., \fI"inet filter iso_clients"\fR). The set will
not be created if it doesn't already exist. \fB\-\-nft\-set\-playground\fR names
the set for playground (typically \fB/24\fR .. \fB/29\fR) clients, that are
not isolated from each other.

.TP
\fB\-\-nft\-set\-playground\-subnet\fR \fIname\fR
Set for the subnet that contains all playground clients.

.TP
\fB\-\-nft\-set\-isolated\fR \fIname\fR
Set for isolated \fB/32\fR clients.

.TP
\fB\-\-nft\-set\-compat\fR \fIname\fR
Set for legacy \fB/30\fR clients.

.TP
\fB\-\-nft\-set\-gateway\fR \fIname\fR
Set for the dynamic gateway IPs created for \fB/30\fR blocks.

.TP
\fB\-\-nft\-set\-network\fR \fIname\fR
Set for the network addresses of \fB/30\fR blocks.

.TP
\fB\-\-nft\-set\-broadcast\fR \fIname\fR
Set for the broadcast addresses of \fB/30\fR blocks.

.TP
\fB\-\-nft\-set\-subnet\fR \fIname\fR
Set for the CIDR blocks of \fB/30\fR allocations. \fBNote:\fR This set must
have the \fBinterval\fR flag enabled in \fBNFTables\fR.

.TP
\fB\-\-static\fR \fImac=ip[,hostname][,compat][,masquerade][,playground]\fR
Map a MAC to a specific IP. Can be repeated.
.br
\fBhostname\fR: (Optional) name for this device. The name is
authoritative: it is recorded in the lease and passed to the hook, and it
takes precedence over whatever the client asks to be called in DHCP
option 12. Clients with no static entry are still named by option 12.
.br
\fBcompat\fR: (Optional) flag to force this device into legacy or
compatibility mode.
.br
\fBmasquerade\fR: (Optional) enable source NAT for this client.
.br
\fBplayground\fR: (Optional) Assign to the shared playground subnet. IP must be
within the calculated playground range.
.br
.B Note:
For \fIcompat\fR entries, the IP must be the ".2" offset of a valid
\fB/30\fR block (e.g., .2, .6, .10). The server will reserve the entire 4-IP
block immediately at startup.
.br
Static entries are initialized immediately at startup. The server configures the
interface, routes, and firewall rules before the client even connects. These
addresses are permanently removed from the free pool.
A reservation is operator state and a client cannot undo it: a DHCPRELEASE
from a statically bound device leaves its addresses, gateway alias and
firewall rules in place.

.TP
\fB\-\-dhcp\-option\fR \fICODE,TYPE,VALUE\fR
Inject custom options into DHCP replies.
.br
\fBTYPE:\fR \fIip\fR, \fIips\fR (list), \fIstr\fR, \fIint8\fR, \fIint16\fR, \fIint32\fR, \fIhex\fR.
.br
\fBVALUE:\fR The data. For \fIhex\fR, colons are optional.

.TP
\fB\-\-playground\-size\fR \fIn\fR
Number of IPs to reserve. Must be > 5. The server rounds up to the next CIDR
boundary (e.g., 50 -> 64 -> /26). If size equals the pool size, the server
behaves like a traditional DHCP server (no isolation).

.TP
\fB\-\-playground\-mac\fR \fImac\fR

.TP
\fB\-\-playground\-oui\fR \fIprefix\fR

.TP
\fB\-\-playground\-vendor\fR \fIstring\fR
Assign matching clients to the playground. An empty string for vendor matches
\fIall\fR clients.

.TP
\fB\-\-hook\-file\fR \fIpath\fR
Path to an executable script (e.g., shell or Python) that is invoked whenever a
lease is added or released. See \fBHOOK SCRIPT\fR below for details.

.TP
\fB\-\-isolation\fR \fI{on,off,system}\fR
In the vast majority of cases, the job of \fBisodhcp\fR is done as soon as it
sends all traffic through the layer 3 router. Policy decisions are supposed to
be enforced by a third-party firewall. \fB\-\-isolation\fR provides minimal
built-in firewall support for the \fBforward\fR chain policy:
.br
\fBon\fR: DROPs intra-subnet traffic except for playground-to-playground.
.br
\fBoff\fR: ACCEPTs intra-subnet traffic.
.br
\fBsystem\fR: Adds no rules and relies on external firewall.

.TP
\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-interval\fR \fIseconds\fR
How often to probe addresses with broadcast ARP to find devices using one
without a lease; see \fBARP SWEEP\fR below. Defaults to 900. At least 60, or 0
to turn the sweep off.

.TP
\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-rate\fR \fIn\fR
Never send more than \fIn\fR broadcast ARP probes per second. Defaults to 2; at most 5.

.TP
\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-max\fR \fIn\fR
Probe at most \fIn\fR addresses per sweep. Defaults to 512.

.TP
\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-candidates\fR \fIk\fR
Also probe the next \fIk\fR standard and \fIk\fR playground addresses
dynamic allocation will hand out, and, unless \fIk\fR is 0, the next /30
block. Defaults to 8.

.TP
\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-full\fR
Also trickle through the rest of the pool when it is larger than a /23.
Smaller pools always are.

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B isodhcp
is a single binary that runs either as the daemon or as a client that inspects
an already-running daemon. When one of the actions below is given, the process
connects to the daemon's socket, prints the answer and exits without touching
the network or the firewall. This needs no elevated privileges beyond access to
the socket, and the daemon computes every answer under its own lock, so the
snapshot is internally consistent and free of races.

.PP
The daemon listens on a read-only \fBAF_UNIX\fR socket (mode \fI0600\fR),
auto-located in the state directory (see \fB\-\-socket\fR and \fBFILES\fR). Only
the user running the daemon and root may connect. The interface given with
\fB\-i\fR selects which daemon is queried, so one client can address any of
several per-interface instances.

.TP
.B \-\-status
Print a health summary: pool utilization and the number of free \fB/30\fR
blocks, client counts by mode, quarantine and rate-limiter saturation,
playground occupancy, uptime and the next lease expiry.

.TP
\fB\-\-verify\fR, \fB\-\-check\fR
Cross-check the daemon's in-memory model against the live kernel and
\fBnftables\fR state it manages, and report any drift. Only isodhcp's own
footprint is examined: its private table \fIisodhcp_<iface>\fR, the named sets
configured with \fB\-\-nft\-set\-*\fR, the in-pool interface aliases, and the
in-pool \fB/32\fR host routes in the main routing table. Foreign interfaces,
address families, routing tables and rules are never inspected, so it is safe on
a multi-homed, dual-stack, policy-routed host. Discrepancies are graded
\fIalarming\fR, \fImoderate\fR, \fIlow\fR or \fIunknown\fR; any that concern a
lease which changed mid-check are tagged \fItransient\fR and ignored. The exit
status is Nagios-compatible:
.RS
.IP 0 4
Clean (no drift, or only low/transient findings).
.IP 1
Warning: only moderate discrepancies, such as stale entries a reload would clear.
.IP 2
Critical: one or more alarming discrepancies (a missing route, alias or SNAT
rule for an active lease, a wrong SNAT target, a missing element in a managed
set, or an internal index desync).
.IP 3
Unknown: the daemon could not be reached, or a managed set/table could not be
read.
.RE

.TP
\fB\-\-dump\fR[=\fIsections\fR]
Dump state tables. \fIsections\fR is an optional comma-separated list of
\fIleases\fR, \fIsubnets\fR, \fIquarantine\fR, \fIratelimit\fR, \fIpool\fR,
\fIconfig\fR and \fIfirewall\fR (default: \fIleases,subnets\fR). The
\fIfirewall\fR section is the unfiltered \fBactual\fR kernel/nftables state as
the daemon sees it \(em host routes with their routing table and type, interface
addresses, SNAT rules, and the elements of every configured set \(em the ground
truth to compare against \fB\-\-verify\fR and \fB\-\-dump leases\fR.

.TP
\fB\-\-show\fR \fIMAC|IP|HOSTNAME\fR
Show a single client and, for each resource it should own (host route or gateway
alias, set membership, SNAT rule), whether that resource is actually present.

.TP
.B \-\-config
Print the effective running configuration: addresses, DNS, lease time, isolation
mode, socket and private-table names, playground geometry, classifier rules and
the configured set names.

.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the selected diagnostic as JSON instead of formatted text. Combine with any
action above; for \fB\-\-verify\fR the exit status is unchanged.

.TP
.B \-\-redact
Mask MAC addresses and omit hostnames in the output, for pasting into tickets or
piping to monitoring.

.TP
.B \-\-all
With \fB\-\-verify\fR, also list discrepancies tagged \fItransient\fR.

.TP
\fB\-\-socket\fR \fIpath\fR
Override the diagnostics socket path. By default the daemon creates, and the
client looks for, \fIisodhcp_<iface>.sock\fR in the systemd \fIStateDirectory\fR
(\fI$STATE_DIRECTORY\fR), then \fI/var/lib/isodhcp\fR, then the current
directory.

.SH ARP SWEEP
A device that uses an address without holding a lease \(em configured
statically, or still using a lease that has lapsed \(em never talks to the
DHCP server, and may send nothing at all for days. It does answer ARP for its
address while it is up, even when otherwise idle. So \fBisodhcp\fR sends ARP
requests, strictly within the pool, for, in this order:
.RS
.IP \- 2
every quarantined address, every address with an open finding, and every
address whose lease ran out in the last seven days;
.IP \- 2
the addresses dynamic allocation will hand out next
(\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-candidates\fR), and the /30 block a new compat client would
get;
.IP \- 2
every leased address, in rotation if they do not all fit;
.IP \- 2
the rest of the pool, a slice per sweep sized for a full pass every four
hours as far as the budget allows, if the pool is no larger than a /23 or
\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-full\fR is given.
.RE
.PP
The first two take at most a quarter of a sweep each. Requests are broadcast,
since a second responder cannot be found otherwise. A device already known to
claim an address that is not leased \(em one with an open finding, or the
former holder of a lease that ran out \(em is also asked by unicast, which a
wireless network acknowledges and retries where a lost broadcast goes
unnoticed. Access points that answer ARP on behalf of their clients (proxy ARP,
ARP suppression) can hide a device they have not learned about.
.PP
Each request is sent from an address the server uses on the target's own
subnet: a compat client's /30 gateway, the playground gateway, or the server
address. The host's own addresses are asked from 0.0.0.0 instead, so that no
request ever announces them. Replies from the host's own MACs are ignored for
its own addresses, and reported for any other.
.PP
Replies to a request also reach the kernel, so asking for a leased address
that a second device answers for can move the kernel's neighbour entry, and
with it the lessee's traffic, to that device. Nobody but the lessee is asked
by unicast for a leased address. Just before the probe, and then every half
lock time (a second by default) until its window closes, the lessee's entry is
refreshed, which makes Linux ignore other devices' replies however late they
come; an entry pinned with a static ARP entry is left alone. Should a reply get
through anyway, the entry is pointed back at the lessee as soon as the probe is
over.
.PP
The sweep only reports. A reply from anybody other than an address's lessee
means:
.RS
.IP \- 2
\fBA free address:\fR the next client given it would share it. The log names
the \fB\-\-static\fR entry that would reserve it for the device, if that is
legitimate, and says so if the device is the former holder of a lease that ran
out.
.IP \- 2
\fBA leased address:\fR an IP conflict. The lease itself is left alone: forcing
a client off its address would let anybody evict any client by answering ARP
for it, and spoofing ARP is exactly what a man in the middle does. The log asks
the operator to establish which device is legitimate first, and only then names
the \fB\-\-static\fR options that would separate them. Two MACs that answer for
each other's addresses may be one host with two interfaces on the segment (on
Linux, net.ipv4.conf.all.arp_ignore=1 stops that). ARP cannot tell that apart
from spoofing, so it remains a finding, but it is logged once, as a warning,
and \fB\-\-verify\fR lists it as low. A lost broadcast does not undo that;
it lasts until one side's finding is cleared.
.IP \- 2
\fBAn address the server uses itself\fR (a /30 gateway, network or broadcast
address): a conflict.
.IP \- 2
\fBA MAC answering for at least eight unleased addresses, and at least half of
those probed in a sweep:\fR a proxy-ARP host, reported as one finding rather
than once per address, and still reported for every leased address it answers
for.
.RE
.PP
Findings are logged at error level, repeated every six hours, and reported as
alarming by \fB\-\-verify\fR until three probes pass without a reply \(em
probes that could have heard one. A probe whose window overflowed with replies
proves nothing; \fB\-\-status\fR counts those in the last sweep and
\fB\-\-verify\fR reports them. A sweep that probed several lessees without
hearing a single one is blind: it clears nothing until it hears again, and
\fB\-\-verify\fR reports that state. Nothing the sweep finds is kept across
restarts.
.PP
The cost is bounded independently of the pool size: at most
\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-max\fR addresses per sweep, never more than
\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-rate\fR broadcasts per second, spread across
\fB\-\-arp\-sweep\-interval\fR with jitter rather than sent as a burst; the
unicast requests to known responders, a few per probe at most, come on top. A
wireless segment normally sends every broadcast on every radio at the basic
rate, waking power-saving clients, which is why it matters. A lightly used /24
at the defaults sees roughly one probe every fifteen to thirty seconds. Every
structure fed by replies is capped too, and so is what is logged, errors from
an allowance of their own. A socket that keeps failing is reopened, and one
that cannot be opened is tried again every interval.

.SH HOOK SCRIPT
If \fB\-\-hook\-file\fR is specified, the server executes the script
asynchronously on lease changes.

.PP
\fBArguments:\fP
.br
$1 = \fBadd\fR or \fBdel\fR
.br
$2 = MAC Address
.br
$3 = IP Address
.br
$4 = Hostname (if available)

.PP
\fBEnvironment Variables:\fP

.TP
.B ISODHCP_MODE
The client mode: \fBstandard\fR, \fBcompat\fR, or \fBplayground\fR.
.TP
.B ISODHCP_PID
Process ID of the server.
.TP
.B ISODHCP_INTERFACE
The listening interface (e.g., \fIguest\fR).
.TP
.B ISODHCP_SERVER_IP
The router's main IP address.
.TP
.B ISODHCP_POOL_CIDR
The network used to allocate the dynamic pool of IP addresses.
.TP
.B ISODHCP_DNS
The DNS server advertised by the DHCP server.
.TP
.B ISODHCP_LEASE_FILE
The JSON file where the server persists its internal state.
.TP
.B ISODHCP_TOTAL_LEASES
Current count of active leases.
.TP
.B ISODHCP_FREE_IPS
Current unassigned IP addresses.
.TP
.B ISODHCP_ISOLATION
Current isolation mode. Can be "on", "off" or "system".
.TP
.B ISODHCP_MASQ
"1" if Masquerade is enabled for this client, else "0".
.TP
.B ISODHCP_COMPAT
"1" if legacy/compat mode is enabled, else "0".
.TP
.B ISODHCP_EXPIRY
Lease expiration timestamp (UNIX epoch) or "inf".
.TP
.B ISODHCP_GATEWAY
The specific gateway IP assigned to this client (varies for /30 clients).
.TP
.B ISODHCP_NETMASK
The specific netmask for this client. Either "255.255.255.255"
or "255.255.255.252".
.TP
.B ISODHCP_SUBNET
The specific subnet assigned to this /30 client.

.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP
The server catches these signals to perform an orderly shutdown. It flushes the
current state of all leases to the JSON file before exiting.

.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Reload configuration state. The server will re-scan the in-memory leases and
re-apply all external system state:
.RS
.IP \- 2
Refresh \fBNFTables\fR sets (add missing elements, but don't remove any
extraneous ones if present).
.IP \- 2
Re-add missing IP aliases to the interface.
.IP \- 2
Sync kernel routing table (restore missing /32 routes).
.RE

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
\fB1. Auto-detected configuration (standard usage)\fP
.br
Start the server on \fIguest0\fR. The server IP and pool are derived from the
interface's system configuration:
.IP
.B isodhcp \-i guest0

.PP
\fB2. Manual override with static IPs\fP
.br
Manually specify the gateway and pool, and assign a static IP to a printer:
.IP
.B isodhcp \-i eth1 \-s 192.168.50.1 \-p 192.168.50.0/24 \-\-static 00:11:22:33:44:55=192.168.50.10,laserprinter

.PP
\fB3. High-turnover guest network\fP
.br
Set a short lease time (5 minutes) for a busy public Wi-Fi interface:
.IP
.B isodhcp \-i wlan0 \-\-lease-time 300

.PP
\fB4. Systemd service definition\fP
.br
A typical \fIExecStart\fR and \fIExecReload\fR line in a systemd unit file:
.IP
.B ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /opt/isodhcp/server.py \-\-interface eth1 \-\-lease-file /opt/isodhcp/leases.json
.IP
.B ExecReload=/bin/kill \-USR1 $MAINPID

.PP
\fB5. Handling legacy devices\fP
.br
Run the server with micro-segmentation for a specific printer, all
Raspberry Pis, and old Windows clients:
.IP
.B isodhcp \-i eth1 \-\-compat\-mac 00:11:22:33:44:55 \-\-compat\-oui B8:27:EB \-\-compat\-vendor "MSFT 5.0"

.PP
\fB6. Masquerading a specific device\fP
.br
Force a device with a specific MAC to be masqueraded behind the gateway IP:
.IP
.B isodhcp \-\-masquerade\-mac aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff

.PP
\fB7. Advanced hybrid configuration\fP
.br
Run on \fIguest0\fR. Treat Nintendo Switch (OUI \fI98:B6:E9\fR) and Windows XP
clients as legacy. Assign a static IP to a client that can't handle /32 routes.
Populate \fBNFTables\fR sets for firewalling:
.IP
.nf
isodhcp \-i guest0 \\
  \-\-compat\-oui 98:B6:E9 \\
  \-\-compat\-vendor "MSFT 5.0" \\
  \-\-static 00:11:22:33:44:55=192.168.2.6,compat,masquerade \\
  \-\-nft\-set\-isolated "inet filter client_iso" \\
  \-\-nft\-set\-compat "inet filter client_legacy" \\
  \-\-nft\-set\-gateway "inet filter local_gateways"
.fi

.PP
\fB8. Hybrid setup with playground\fP
.br
Run on \fIguest\fR. Isolate most clients, but provide a 32-IP playground for
Nintendo Switch consoles (OUI 98:B6:E9) and Apple TVs (Vendor "tvOS").
Masquerade the consoles. Advertise an NTP server at "192.168.1.1".
.IP
.nf
isodhcp \-i guest \-\-playground\-size 32 \\
  \-\-playground\-oui 98:B6:E9 \\
  \-\-playground\-vendor "tvOS" \\
  \-\-masquerade\-oui 98:B6:E9 \\
  \-\-dhcp\-option "42,ip,192.168.1.1"
.fi

.PP
\fB9. Unifi controller DHCP option\fP
.br
Send option 43 (vendor specific) to Ubiquiti devices pointing to
controller 192.168.1.10:
.IP
.B isodhcp \-\-dhcp\-option "43,hex,01:04:C0:A8:01:0A"

.PP
\fB10. Static legacy reservation\fP
.br
Assign a printer to 10.100.0.10. Force it to use a /30 subnet (occupying
10.100.0.8/30) because it doesn't support /32:
.IP
.B isodhcp \-\-static 00:11:22:33:44:55=10.100.0.10,printer,compat

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./dhcp_leases_{INTERFACE}.json
The default location for lease persistence. Ensure the user running the daemon
has write permissions to this file (or directory).
.TP
.I <state-dir>/isodhcp_{INTERFACE}.sock
The read-only diagnostics socket (mode \fI0600\fR) created by the daemon and
used by client-mode invocations. \fI<state-dir>\fR is the systemd
\fIStateDirectory\fR if set, otherwise \fI/var/lib/isodhcp\fR or the current
directory. Overridable with \fB\-\-socket\fR.

.SH SECURITY & PERMISSIONS
To run \fBisodhcp\fR as an unprivileged user, the process requires the
following Linux capabilities:

.TP
.B CAP_NET_RAW
Required to open raw sockets for sending/receiving DHCP packets via Scapy.

.TP
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
Required to modify the kernel routing table (adding/removing /32 routes via
\fBnetlink\fR).

.TP
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
Required to bind to port 67 (optional, depending on implementation
details, but recommended).

.PP
In a systemd unit file, these can be assigned via:
.br
\fIAmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_RAW CAP_NET_ADMIN CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE\fR

.SH NFTABLES CONFIGURATION
To use the \fB\-\-nft\-set\-subnet\fR feature, the target set must be capable
of storing intervals (CIDRs). Example configuration in \fI/etc/nftables.conf\fR:

.nf
table inet filter {
    set iso_clients { type ipv4_addr; }
    set compat_subnets {
        type ipv4_addr
        flags interval
    }
}
.fi

.SH AVAHI / MDNS INTERACTION
By default, this server disables \fBPromiscuous Mode\fR on the raw socket to
prevent the OS from flagging a "link change" event. This prevents local mDNS
daemons (like Avahi) from flushing their cache every time the server restarts.

To ensure proper mDNS reflection between the isolated network and a trusted
network, ensure the router interface has a valid IP address within the pool
range (e.g., \fI10.100.0.1/24\fR), even though clients are assigned \fI/30\fR
and \fI/32\fR masks.

